        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "Now, moving on to the second part: proving whether this statement is true or false in the domain of positive integers."
      },
      {
        "label": "adding-knowledge",
        "text": "First, let's recall what prime numbers are. A prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself. So, primes are numbers like 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, etc.\n\nNow, what about even numbers? An even number is any integer that is divisible by 2. So, even numbers are 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, etc."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "So, the intersection of prime numbers and even numbers would be numbers that are both prime and even. Let's think about that. The only even prime number is 2, because any other even number is divisible by 2, hence not prime."
      },
      {
        "label": "example-testing",
