is divisible by 3.\" So, for reflexivity, it's only when a = b. So, if a ≠ b, then (a, b) is not in R. So, for reflexivity, we only need to check when a = b. So, in this case, only when a = 3 or 6, (a, a) is in R. So, reflexivity fails because not all elements satisfy it. So, R is not reflexive.\n\nBut wait, hold on. The definition is (a, b) ∈ R if and only if a + b is divisible by 3. So, for reflexivity, we need (a, a) ∈ R for all a in S. Since for a = 1, 2, 4, 5, (a, a) is not in R, so reflexivity fails. So, R is not reflexive.\n\n**Checking Symmetry:**\n\nSymmetry requires that if (a, b) ∈ R, then (b, a) ∈ R. Since a + b is divisible by 3, then b + a is also divisible by 3 because addition is commutative. So, if a + b is divisible by 3, then b + a is also divisible by 3. Therefore, symmetry holds.\n\n**Checking Transitivity:**\n\nThis is the tricky part. I need to check if for all a, b, c in S, if (a, b) ∈ R and (b, c) ∈ R, then (a, c) ∈ R. So, if a + b is divisible by 3 and b + c is divisible by 3, then a + c must be divisible by 3.\n\nLet me think about how to approach this. Maybe I can look at the residues of the numbers modulo 3. Since divisibility by 3 is related to the remainder when divided by 3.\n\nLet me compute the residues of each element in S modulo 3:\n\n- 1 mod 3 = 1\n- 2 mod 3 = 2\n- 3 mod 3 = 0\n- 4 mod 3 = 1 (since 4 - 3 = 1)\n- 5 mod 3 = 2 (since 5 - 3 = 2)\n- 6 mod 3 = 0\n\nSo, the residues are: {1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 0}.\n\nNow, for (a, b) ∈ R, a + b ≡ 0 mod 3. So, the sum of their residues must be 0 mod 3.\n\nSimilarly, for (b, c) ∈ R, b + c ≡ 0 mod 3.\n\nWe need to see if a + c ≡ 0 mod 3.\n\nLet me consider all possible cases for a, b, c based on their residues.\n\nCase 1: a ≡ 0 mod 3, b ≡ 0 mod 3.\n\nThen, a + b ≡ 0 + 0 ≡ 0 mod 3. So, (a, b) ∈ R.\n\nSimilarly, b + c ≡ 0 + c ≡ 0 mod 3. So, c ≡ 0 mod 3.\n\nTherefore, a + c ≡ 0 + 0 ≡ 0 mod 3. So, (a, c) ∈ R.\n\nCase 2: a ≡ 1 mod 3, b ≡ 2 mod 3.\n\nThen, a + b ≡ 1 + 2 ≡ 0 mod 3. So, (a, b) ∈ R.\n\nSimilarly, b + c ≡ 2 + c ≡ 0 mod 3. So, c ≡ 1 mod 3.\n\nTherefore, a + c ≡ 1 + 1 ≡ 2 mod 3. Which is not 0 mod 3. So, (a, c) ∉ R.\n\nWait, so in this case, if a ≡ 1 and b ≡ 2, and b ≡ 2 and c ≡ 1, then a + c ≡ 2 mod 3, which is not 0. So, (a, c) is not in R.\n\nTherefore, transitivity fails in this case.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me double-check.\n\nIf a ≡ 1, b ≡ 2, c ≡ 1.\n\nThen, a + b = 1 + 2 = 3 ≡ 0 mod 3, so (a, b) ∈ R.\n\nb + c = 2 + 1 = 3 ≡ 0 mod 3, so (b, c) ∈ R.\n\nBut a + c = 1 + 1 = 2 ≡ 2 mod 3, which is not 0. So, (a, c) ∉ R.\n\nTherefore, transitivity fails because we have a case where (a, b) ∈ R and (b, c) ∈ R, but (a, c) ∉ R.\n\nSo, transitivity does not hold for this relation.\n\nWait, but hold on. Is this always the case? Let me see.\n\nSuppose a ≡ 1, b ≡ 2, c ≡ 1.\n\nThen, a + b ≡ 0, b + c ≡ 0, but a + c ≡ 2.\n\nAlternatively, if a ≡ 1, b ≡ 2, c ≡ 2.\n\nThen, a + b ≡ 0, b + c ≡ 1, which is not 0. So, (b, c) ∉ R.\n\nWait, so in this case, if a ≡ 1, b ≡ 2, c ≡ 2, then (a, b) ∈ R, but (b, c) ∉ R, so transitivity doesn't apply here.\n\nBut in the previous case, when a ≡ 1, b ≡ 2, c ≡ 1, then (a, b) ∈ R and (b, c) ∈ R, but (a, c) ∉ R. So, transitivity fails.\n\nTherefore, transitivity does not hold for this relation.\n\nWait, but is there a case where (a, b) ∈ R and (b, c) ∈ R, but (a, c) ∉ R?\n\nYes, as shown above.\n\nTherefore, transitivity fails.\n\nSo, since reflexivity fails, the relation R is not an equivalence relation.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure I didn't miss anything.\n\nWait, another thought: Maybe I should check all possible triples to see if transitivity holds.\n\nBut that might take a while. Alternatively, perhaps I can think in terms of the residues.\n\nSince the relation is based on the sum being divisible by 3, which is equivalent to the sum of residues being 0 mod 3.\n\nSo, if a ≡ x, b ≡ y, c ≡ z mod 3, then (a, b) ∈ R iff x + y ≡ 0 mod 3, and (b, c) ∈ R iff y + z ≡ 0 mod 3. Then, (a, c) ∈ R iff x + z ≡ 0 mod 3.\n\nSo, if x + y ≡ 0 and y + z ≡ 0, then x + z ≡ (x + y) + (y + z) - 2y ≡ 0 + 0 - 2y ≡ -2y mod 3.\n\nBut since x + y ≡ 0, x ≡ -y mod 3. Similarly, z ≡ -y mod 3.\n\nTherefore, x + z ≡ (-y) + (-y) ≡ -2y mod 3.\n\nBut unless y ≡ 0 mod 3, x + z ≡ -2y mod 3. So, unless y ≡ 0, x + z ≡ -2y mod 3.\n\nBut if y ≡ 0, then x + z ≡ 0 mod 3, so (a, c) ∈ R.\n\nBut if y ≡ 1 or 2, then x + z ≡ -2y mod 3.\n\nIf y ≡ 1, then x + z ≡ -2 ≡ 1 mod 3.\n\nIf y ≡ 2, then x + z ≡ -4 ≡ 2 mod 3.\n\nSo, in either case, x + z ≡ 1 or 2 mod 3, which is not 0. Therefore, (a, c) ∉ R.\n\nTherefore, whenever y ≡ 1 or 2, x + z ≡ 1 or 2 mod 3, so (a, c) ∉ R.\n\nTherefore, transitivity fails because we can have (a, b) ∈ R and (b, c) ∈ R, but (a, c) ∉ R.\n\nTherefore, transitivity does not hold.\n\nSo, since reflexivity fails, R is not an equivalence relation.\n\nBut wait, let me check reflexivity again. For reflexivity, we need (a, a) ∈ R for all a in S. But as we saw, only when a ≡ 0 mod 3, which are 3 and 6, does (a, a) ∈ R. For a = 1, 2, 4, 5, (a, a) ∉ R. So, reflexivity fails.\n\nTherefore, R is not an equivalence relation.\n\nBut just to make sure, let me think if there's another way to approach this.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think about the equivalence classes.\n\nIf R is an equivalence relation, then it should partition S into disjoint equivalence classes where each class consists of elements related to each other.\n\nBut since R is not an equivalence relation, it doesn't have to partition S, but it's not even an equivalence relation.\n\nBut let me try to see what the equivalence classes would be if R were an equivalence relation.\n\nWait, but since R is not an equivalence relation, the classes might not be well-defined.\n\nBut perhaps I can still try to see.\n\nIf R were an equivalence relation, then the equivalence classes would be the sets of elements that are related to each other.\n\nBut since R is not reflexive, symmetric, or transitive, the classes might not be consistent.\n\nBut maybe I can try to see.\n\nWait, let me think about the elements:\n\nElements in S: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.\n\nCompute the residues mod 3:\n\n1: 1\n\n2: 2\n\n3: 0\n\n4: 1\n\n5: 2\n\n6: 0\n\nSo, the residues are {0, 1, 2}.\n\nIf R were an equivalence relation, the equivalence classes would be based on the sum of residues.\n\nBut since R is not an equivalence relation, the classes might not be consistent.\n\nBut perhaps I can try to see.\n\nWait, maybe I can think of the relation as a graph where each node is an element of S, and there is an edge from a to b if a + b is divisible by 3.\n\nThen, the equivalence classes would be the connected components of this graph.\n\nBut since R is not an equivalence relation, the graph might have multiple connected components, but since it's not even reflexive, the graph might not have loops.\n\nWait, but in this case, since R is not reflexive, there are no loops, so the graph is actually a simple graph without loops.\n\nBut in any case, let's try to see.\n\nLet me try to draw the graph.\n\nNodes: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.\n\nEdges:\n\n1 is connected to b where 1 + b ≡ 0 mod 3. So, b ≡ 2 mod 3. So, 2 and 5.\n\n2 is connected to b where 2 + b ≡ 0 mod 3. So, b ≡ 1 mod 3. So, 1, 4.\n\n3 is connected to b where 3 + b ≡ 0 mod 3. So, b ≡ 0 mod 3. So, 3 and 6.\n\n4 is connected to b where 4 + b ≡ 0 mod 3. So, b ≡ 2 mod 3. So, 2 and 5.\n\n5 is connected to b where 5 + b ≡ 0 mod 3. So, b ≡ 1 mod 3. So, 1 and 4.\n\n6 is connected to b where 6 + b ≡ 0 mod 3. So, b ≡ 0 mod 3. So, 3 and 6.\n\nSo, the edges are:\n\n1 ↔ 2, 1 ↔ 4\n\n2 ↔ 1, 2 ↔ 5\n\n3 ↔ 6\n\n4 ↔ 1, 4 ↔ 5\n\n5 ↔ 1, 5 ↔ 4\n\n6 ↔ 3, 6 ↔ 3\n\nSo, let's see the connected components.\n\nStarting with 1: connected to 2 and 4. 2 is connected to 5, which is connected to 4. So, 1, 2, 4, 5 are all connected.\n\n3 is connected to 6, which is connected to itself. So, 3 and 6 form another connected component.\n\nTherefore, the graph has two connected components: {1, 2, 4, 5} and {3, 6}.\n\nBut since R is not an equivalence relation, these connected components are not equivalence classes. Equivalence classes would require that if a ~ b, then b ~ a, and transitivity, etc. But since R is not an equivalence relation, these connected components don't satisfy the properties.\n\nBut wait, in this case, the connected components are the equivalence classes if R were an equivalence relation. But since R is not, the classes are not well-defined.\n\nBut in any case, since R is not an equivalence relation, it doesn't satisfy all three properties.\n\nTherefore, R is not an equivalence relation.\n\nBut just to make sure, let me check if the relation is symmetric and transitive, even though reflexivity fails.\n\nWait, symmetry: If (a, b) ∈ R, then (b, a) ∈ R. Since a + b = b + a, which is the same, so yes, symmetry holds.\n\nTransitivity: As we saw earlier, if (a, b) ∈ R and (b, c) ∈ R, then (a, c) ∉ R in some cases, so transitivity fails.\n\nTherefore, R is not an equivalence relation.\n\nSo, to summarize:\n\n- Reflexivity fails because not all elements satisfy a + a ≡ 0 mod 3.\n\n- Symmetry holds because a + b ≡ 0 mod 3 implies b + a ≡ 0 mod 3.\n\n- Transitivity fails because there exist a, b, c such that (a, b) ∈ R and (b, c) ∈ R, but (a, c) ∉ R.\n\nTherefore, R is not an equivalence relation.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe relation \\( R \\) is not an equivalence relation. The final answer is \\boxed{R \\text{ is not an equivalence relation}}.\n</think>\n\nTo determine if the relation \\( R \\) on the set \\( S = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\} \\) defined by \\( (a, b) \\in R \\) if and only if \\( a + b \\) is divisible by 3 is an equivalence relation, we need to check if it satisfies reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity.\n\n1. **Reflexivity**: For every element \\( a \\) in \\( S \\), the pair \\( (a, a) \\) must be in \\( R \\). This means \\( a + a \\) should be divisible by 3 for all \\( a \\) in \\( S \\). Checking each element:\n   - \\( 1 + 1 = 2 \\) (not divisible by 3)\n   - \\( 2 + 2 = 4 \\) (not divisible by 3)\n   - \\( 3 + 3 = 6 \\) (divisible by 3)\n   - \\( 4 + 4 = 8 \\) (not divisible by 3)\n   - \\( 5 + 5 = 10 \\) (not divisible by 3)\n   - \\( 6 + 6 = 12 \\) (divisible by 3)\n   Since not all elements satisfy the condition, reflexivity fails.\n\n2. **Symmetry**: If \\( (a, b) \\in R \\), then \\( (b, a) \\) must also be in \\( R \\). Since addition is commutative, \\( a + b = b + a \\), so symmetry holds.\n\n3. **Transitivity**: If \\( (a, b) \\in R \\) and \\( (b, c) \\in R \\), then \\( (a, c) \\) must be in \\( R \\). Checking if \\( a + c \\) is divisible by 3:\n   - Consider \\( a \\equiv 1 \\mod 3 \\), \\( b \\equiv 2 \\mod 3 \\), \\( c \\equiv 1 \\mod 3 \\):\n     - \\( a + b \\equiv 0 \\mod 3 \\)\n     - \\( b + c \\equiv 0 \\mod 3 \\)\n     - \\( a + c \\equiv 2 \\mod 3 \\) (not divisible by 3)\n   This shows transitivity fails because \\( (a, b) \\in R \\) and \\( (b, c) \\in R \\) but \\( (a, c) \\notin R \\).\n\nSince reflexivity fails, the relation \\( R \\) is not an equivalence relation.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{R \\text{ is not an equivalence relation}}\n\\]",
