∪ B) - C.\n\nTake any element x in (A - C) ∪ (B - C). This means that x is either in A - C or in B - C. So, there are two cases again:\n\nCase 1: x is in A - C. Then, x is in A and x is not in C. Since x is in A, it is certainly in A ∪ B, and since x is not in C, x is in (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nCase 2: x is in B - C. Similarly, x is in B and x is not in C. Therefore, x is in B ∪ A (since union is commutative), and x is not in C, so x is in (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nIn both cases, x is in (A ∪ B) - C. Therefore, every element of (A - C) ∪ (B - C) is also in (A ∪ B) - C, which proves the second inclusion.\n\nSince both inclusions have been established, we can conclude that (A ∪ B) - C equals (A - C) ∪ (B - C). \n\nWait, let me double-check if I missed anything. I considered all possible cases for x being in A or B, and in each case, I showed that x must be in one of the sets on the right-hand side. Also, for the second inclusion, I considered x being in A or B, and in each case, it's in the union on the left-hand side. Yeah, that seems solid. I don't think I made any logical errors here.\n\nJust to be thorough, let me think about a concrete example. Suppose A = {1, 2}, B = {2, 3}, and C = {3, 4}. Then, A ∪ B = {1, 2, 3}, so (A ∪ B) - C = {1, 2} - {3, 4} = {1, 2}. On the other hand, A - C = {1}, B - C = {2}, so (A - C) ∪ (B - C) = {1, 2}. They are equal. \n\nAnother example: Let A = {a, b}, B = {b, c}, C = {c, d}. Then, A ∪ B = {a, b, c}, so (A ∪ B) - C = {a, b} - {c, d} = {a, b}. A - C = {a}, B - C = {b}, so (A - C) ∪ (B - C) = {a, b}. Again, equal.\n\nWait, what if C is empty? Then, (A ∪ B) - C = A ∪ B, and (A - C) ∪ (B - C) = A ∪ B as well. So, equality holds.\n\nWhat if C is equal to A ∪ B? Then, (A ∪ B) - C is empty, and (A - C) ∪ (B - C) is also empty. So, equality holds.\n\nWhat if C is a subset of A? Let's say A = {1, 2}, B = {3, 4}, C = {1}. Then, A ∪ B = {1, 2, 3, 4}, so (A ∪ B) - C = {2, 3, 4}. On the other hand, A - C = {2}, B - C = {3, 4}, so (A - C) ∪ (B - C) = {2, 3, 4}. Equal again.\n\nHmm, seems consistent. I think my proof is solid.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equality is proven, so \\boxed{(A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)}.\n</think>\n\nTo prove that \\((A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\), we will show mutual subset inclusion.\n\nFirst, we show that \\((A \\cup B) - C \\subseteq (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\n1. Take any element \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\). By definition, \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\).\n2. Since \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\), \\(x\\) is in \\(A\\) or \\(B\\).\n3. If \\(x \\in A\\), then \\(x \\in A - C\\), so \\(x \\in (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n4. If \\(x \\in B\\), then \\(x \\in B - C\\), so \\(x \\in (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\nThus, every element of \\((A \\cup B) - C\\) is in \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\), proving \\((A \\cup B) - C \\subseteq (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\nNext, we show that \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C) \\subseteq (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n\n1. Take any element \\(x \\in (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\). This means \\(x\\) is in \\(A - C\\) or \\(x\\) is in \\(B - C\\).\n2. If \\(x \\in A - C\\), then \\(x \\in A\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\). Hence, \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), so \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n3. If \\(x \\in B - C\\), then \\(x \\in B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\). Hence, \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), so \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n\nThus, every element of \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\) is in \\((A \\cup B) - C\\), proving \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C) \\subseteq (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n\nSince both inclusions have been established, we conclude that \\((A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)}\n\\]",
