r any function \\( g \\in S \\), \\( g(1) + g(2) + g(3) = g(1) + g(2) + g(3) \\), which is trivially true. Hence, \\( (g, g) \\in T \\).\n\n2. **Symmetry**: If \\( (g, h) \\in T \\), then \\( g(1) + g(2) + g(3) = h(1) + h(2) + h(3) \\). This implies \\( h(1) + h(2) + h(3) = g(1) + g(2) + g(3) \\), so \\( (h, g) \\in T \\).\n\n3. **Transitivity**: If \\( (g, h) \\in T \\) and \\( (h, k) \\in T \\), then \\( g(1) + g(2) + g(3) = h(1) + h(2) + h(3) \\) and \\( h(1) + h(2) + h(3) = k(1) + k(2) + k(3) \\). Therefore, \\( g(1) + g(2) + g(3) = k(1) + k(2) + k(3) \\), so \\( (g, k) \\in T \\).\n\nThus, \\( T \\) is an equivalence relation.\n\nNext, we determine the number of distinct equivalence classes. The possible sums of the outputs of functions in \\( S \\) range from 3 to 9, giving 7 distinct sums. Therefore, there are 7 equivalence classes.\n\nFor the equivalence class containing the function \\( f \\) where \\( f(1) = f(2) = f(3) = 2 \\), we need to count the number of functions \\( g \\) such that \\( g(1) + g(2) + g(3) = 6 \\). The number of such functions is found by solving \\( x + y + z = 3 \\) and \\( x + 2y + 3z = 6 \\), leading to 7 solutions. Hence, the size of this equivalence class is 7.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe number of distinct equivalence classes is \\boxed{7}, and the size of the equivalence class containing \\( f \\) is \\boxed{7}.",
