nder, so the cross-section is a circle with radius 3 meters. The water level is halfway up the circular face, so it's a semicircle. The volume of water is the area of the semicircle multiplied by the length of the cylinder.\n\nWait, but the problem doesn't specify the length of the cylinder. Hmm, that's a bit confusing. Wait, the tank is a cylinder, but it's lying on its side, so the height of the cylinder is 8 meters. But when it's lying on its side, the height of the cylinder is actually the length of the cylinder. So, the length is 8 meters.\n\nSo, the volume of water when lying on its side is the area of the semicircle (which is (1/2)*π*r²) multiplied by the length of the cylinder, which is 8 meters.\n\nSo, the area of the semicircle is (1/2)*π*(3)^2 = (1/2)*π*9 = (9/2)*π. Then, multiplying by the length, 8 meters, gives the volume as (9/2)*π*8 = 36π cubic meters.\n\nWait, is that right? Let me double-check. The area of a semicircle is indeed (1/2)*π*r², so that's correct. Then, multiplying by the length, which is 8 meters, gives the volume. So, 36π cubic meters.\n\nNow, when the tank is rotated 90 degrees, it stands upright with the circular faces horizontal. So, the tank is now a vertical cylinder, and the water will form a segment of the circle. I need to find the height of the water from the bottom of the tank.\n\nSo, the volume of water remains the same, 36π cubic meters. When the tank is upright, the volume of water is the area of the circular segment times the radius of the cylinder.\n\nWait, no. When the tank is upright, the cross-section is a circle, and the water forms a segment. The volume is the area of the segment multiplied by the length of the cylinder, which is 8 meters.\n\nSo, I need to find the area of the segment when the volume is 36π. Then, I can solve for the height.\n\nBut first, let me recall the formula for the area of a circular segment. The area of a segment is given by (r²/2)(θ - sinθ), where θ is the central angle in radians corresponding to the segment.\n\nBut to find θ, I need to know the height of the segment. Wait, but in this case, the height of the segment is the height of the water from the bottom of the tank. Let me denote that height as h. So, the area of the segment is (r²/2)(θ - sinθ), and the volume is that area times the length, which is 8 meters.\n\nSo, the volume is 36π = (r²/2)(θ - sinθ)*8.\n\nGiven that r is 3 meters, so plugging in:\n\n36π = (9/2)(θ - sinθ)*8\n\nSimplify the right side:\n\n(9/2)*8 = 36, so 36*(θ - sinθ) = 36π\n\nDivide both sides by 36:\n\nθ - sinθ = π\n\nWait, that seems interesting. So, θ - sinθ = π.\n\nBut θ is the central angle in radians. Let me think about this. The equation θ - sinθ = π is a transcendental equation, meaning it can't be solved algebraically. So, I might need to use numerical methods or approximate the solution.\n\nBut before I proceed, let me verify if my reasoning is correct.\n\nSo, when the tank is lying on its side, the water forms a semicircle, which is 180 degrees or π radians. When it's upright, the water forms a segment, and the volume is the same. So, the volume of the segment is 36π, which is equal to the area of the segment times the length of the cylinder.\n\nWait, but hold on, the length of the cylinder is 8 meters, so the volume is 36π = (Area of segment) * 8.\n\nSo, Area of segment = 36π / 8 = 4.5π.\n\nBut the area of the segment is (r²/2)(θ - sinθ). So, plugging in r = 3:\n\nArea = (9/2)(θ - sinθ) = 4.5π\n\nSo, (9/2)(θ - sinθ) = 4.5π\n\nMultiply both sides by 2/9:\n\nθ - sinθ = (4.5π)*(2/9) = (9π/2)*(2/9) = π\n\nSo, θ - sinθ = π\n\nSo, θ - sinθ = π\n\nHmm, so θ = π + sinθ\n\nBut θ is the central angle in radians. Let me think about the possible values of θ.\n\nSince the tank is a cylinder, the maximum central angle for a segment is π radians (180 degrees). But in this case, θ - sinθ = π, which is approximately 3.1416.\n\nBut θ is less than π, because the segment can't be more than a semicircle. Wait, but θ - sinθ = π, which is greater than π, because sinθ is positive for θ between 0 and π.\n\nWait, that can't be. If θ is less than π, then θ - sinθ is less than π, because sinθ is positive. So, θ - sinθ < π.\n\nBut according to the equation, θ - sinθ = π, which is impossible because θ - sinθ is less than π.\n\nHmm, that suggests that my initial assumption is wrong.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in calculating the volume.\n\nWait, when the tank is lying on its side, the water is filling up to the halfway mark, which is 3 meters. So, the cross-sectional area is a semicircle, which is (1/2)*π*r² = (1/2)*π*9 = 4.5π. Then, the volume is 4.5π * 8 = 36π, which is correct.\n\nBut when the tank is upright, the water forms a segment, and the volume is the area of the segment times the length, which is 8 meters. So, 36π = Area of segment * 8, so Area of segment = 4.5π.\n\nBut the area of the segment is (r²/2)(θ - sinθ) = (9/2)(θ - sinθ) = 4.5π.\n\nSo, (9/2)(θ - sinθ) = 4.5π\n\nMultiply both sides by 2/9:\n\nθ - sinθ = (4.5π)*(2/9) = (9π/2)*(2/9) = π\n\nSo, θ - sinθ = π\n\nBut as I thought earlier, θ is less than π, so θ - sinθ is less than π, which is a contradiction.\n\nWait, so that suggests that my initial assumption is wrong. Maybe the water doesn't form a semicircle when the tank is upright? Or perhaps I made a mistake in calculating the volume.\n\nWait, let me think again. When the tank is lying on its side, the water is filling up to the halfway mark, which is 3 meters. So, the cross-section is a semicircle, so the area is 4.5π, and the volume is 4.5π * 8 = 36π.\n\nBut when the tank is upright, the water will form a segment, but the height of the segment is h, which is the height of the water from the bottom. So, the area of the segment is (r²/2)(θ - sinθ), and the volume is that area times the length, which is 8.\n\nSo, 36π = (9/2)(θ - sinθ)*8\n\nSo, 36π = 36(θ - sinθ)\n\nDivide both sides by 36:\n\nπ = θ - sinθ\n\nSo, θ - sinθ = π\n\nBut as I thought earlier, θ is the central angle, wh