ut the prisms are prisms, so they have thickness in the direction perpendicular to their axis. So, Prism 1 has thickness in the y-direction, and Prism 2 has thickness in the y-direction as well.\n\nWait, no. The thickness of the prism is in the direction perpendicular to its axis. So, Prism 1's axis is along the z-axis, so its thickness is in the y-direction. Prism 2's axis is along the x-axis, so its thickness is in the y-direction as well.\n\nSo, both prisms have a thickness of 6 in the y-direction, but Prism 1 is at x=0 to x=6, z=0 to z=10, and Prism 2 is at x=8 to x=2, z=5 to z=15, with its center at (8, 2√3, 5).\n\nWait, but Prism 2 is moving along the x-axis towards Prism 1. So, as it moves, its x-coordinate decreases, but its z-coordinate remains at 5, and its y-coordinate remains at 2√3.\n\nWait, but the prisms are prisms, so they have thickness in the y-direction as well. So, Prism 2's cross-section is an equilateral triangle with side length 6, so in the y-direction, it extends from y=2√3 - 3 to y=2√3 + 3, since the centroid is at (8, 2√3, 5). Wait, no, the centroid of an equilateral triangle is at a distance of (2/3) height from each side.\n\nWait, the height of the equilateral triangle is (sqrt(3)/2)*6 = 3√3. So, the centroid is at 1/3 of the height from each side, so 3√3 / 3 = √3 from each side. So, the y-coordinate of the centroid is 2√3, so the triangle extends from y=2√3 - √3 = √3 to y=2√3 + √3 = 3√3.\n\nSo, Prism 2's cross-section in the y-direction is from y=√3 to y=3√3, and its cross-section in the z-direction is from z=5 to z=15.\n\nPrism 1's cross-section in the y-direction is from y=0 to y=6, since it's an equilateral triangle with side length 6, so the centroid is at (3, 3√3, 0), so the triangle extends from y=0 to y=6.\n\nWait, no. Wait, the base of Prism 1 is in the xy-plane with vertices at (0,0,0), (6,0,0), and (3, 3√3, 0). So, the centroid is at (3, (3√3)/3, 0) = (3, √3, 0). So, the triangle extends from y=0 to y=6, but the centroid is at y=√3.\n\nWait, but the cross-section is an equilateral triangle, so the height is 3√3, so the triangle extends from y=0 to y=6, but the centroid is at y=√3.\n\nWait, no, the centroid is at y=√3, but the triangle extends from y=0 to y=6, so the distance from the centroid to each side is √3, which is correct because the height is 3√3, so 1/3 of that is √3.\n\nSo, Prism 1's cross-section is from y=0 to y=6, and Prism 2's cross-section is from y=√3 to y=3√3.\n\nSo, when Prism 2 moves towards Prism 1, its x-coordinate decreases, but its y and z coordinates remain fixed. So, as it moves, it's moving along the x-axis towards Prism 1, which is at x=0, y=0, z=0.\n\nWait, but the prisms are moving along the x-axis, so their positions are changing in the x-direction, but their y and z positions are fixed. So, the movement is purely along the x-axis.\n\nSo, the problem is to find the x-coordinate where the two prisms first touch each other as Prism 2 moves towards Prism 1.\n\nSo, I need to model the positions of both prisms as functions of x, and find the point where their surfaces touch.\n\nLet me try to model the prisms.\n\nFirst, Prism 1 is fixed at x=0, y=0, z=0, extending up to x=6, y=0, z=10, and y=6, z=0 to z=10.\n\nWait, no. Wait, Prism 1 is a triangular prism with a triangular base in the xy-plane, so its cross-section is in the y-z plane. So, the base is in the xy-plane, so x ranges from 0 to 6, y=0, z=0 to 10. Wait, no, that can't be, because the base is in the xy-plane, so x and y are fixed, but z varies.\n\nWait, no, the base is in the xy-plane, so x and y are fixed, but z varies. So, the base is a triangle with vertices at (0,0,0), (6,0,0), and (3, 3√3, 0). So, the prism extends from z=0 to z=10. So, the entire prism is from x=0 to x=6, y=0 to y=6, and z=0 to z=10.\n\nWait, no, that's not correct. Wait, the prism is a triangular prism, so it's a translation of the triangular base along the z-axis. So, the base is in the xy-plane, and the prism extends along the z-axis. So, the x and y coordinates are fixed for the base, but z varies.\n\nSo, Prism 1 is from x=0 to x=6, y=0 to y=6, and z=0 to z=10.\n\nPrism 2 is moving along the x-axis. Its center is at (8, 2√3, 5), and it's facing the negative x-direction, so it's extending in the negative x-direction. Its cross-section is an equilateral triangle with side length 6, so in the y-z plane, it extends from y=√3 to y=3√3, and from z=5 to z=15.\n\nWait, but as it moves, its x-coordinate decreases. So, the position of Prism 2 at any time t is (8 - t, 2√3, 5), where t is the distance it has moved along the x-axis towards Prism 1.\n\nWait, but actually, the movement is along the x-axis, so the x-coordinate decreases by t, and the y and z coordinates remain the same. So, the position of Prism 2 at time t is (8 - t, 2√3, 5).\n\nNow, I need to find the point where the two prisms first touch each other as t increases from 0.\n\nSo, the prisms will touch when the distance between their surfaces is zero. So, I need to find the minimal t where the closest points between the two prisms are at a distance of zero.\n\nSo, let's model the prisms mathematically.\n\nPrism 1 is fixed at x=0 to x=6, y=0 to y=6, z=0 to z=10.\n\nPrism 2 is moving along the x-axis, so its position is (8 - t, 2√3, 5). Its cross-section is an equilateral triangle in the y-z plane, centered at (2√3, 5), with side length 6, so it extends from y=√3 to y=3√3, and from z=5 to z=15.\n\nWait, but as it moves, its x-coordinate decreases, so the position is (8 - t, 2√3, 5). So, the prism is moving along the x-axis, but its y and z coordinates are fixed.\n\nWait, but the prism is a triangular prism, so it's a translation of the triangular base along the x-axis. So, the base is at x=8 - t, and the prism extends from x=8 - t to x=8 - t + 6, which is x=14 - t.\n\nWait, but Prism 1 is from x=0 to x=6, so as Prism 2 moves, it's moving from x=8 to x=2, but its x-coordinate is 8 - t, so when t=0, it's at x=8, and as t increases, it moves