ful. So, that's a problem.\n\nSo, actually, Combination 2 is invalid because it leads to a contradiction.\n\nSo, going back, the only valid combinations are Combination 4 and Combination 2? Wait, no, Combination 2 is invalid because it leads to a contradiction.\n\nWait, let me re-examine Combination 2.\n\nCombination 2: A=T, B=T, C=F.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=T, C=F, so implication is false. So, Alice's statement is false. But in Combination 2, Alice is truthful. So, contradiction.\n\nTherefore, Combination 2 is invalid.\n\nSo, only Combination 4 is valid.\n\nWait, but earlier, I thought Combination 4 was valid because Ben's statement was true, but let me check again.\n\nCombination 4: A=T, B=F, C=F.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=F, so implication is true.\n- Ben's statement: A ⊕ B. A=T, B=F, so true.\n- Clara's statement: A ↔ C. A=T, C=F, so false.\n\nSo, all three statements are true except Clara's. But since Clara is lying, her statement is false, which is consistent.\n\nWait, but Ben's statement is true, which is consistent because Ben is truthful.\n\nSo, Combination 4 is consistent.\n\nWait, but earlier, I thought Combination 2 was invalid because Ben's statement would be false, but in Combination 2, Ben is truthful, so his statement must be true. So, Combination 2 is invalid.\n\nSo, only Combination 4 is valid.\n\nWait, but let me check Combination 3 again.\n\nCombination 3: A=T, B=F, C=T.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=F, so implication is true.\n- Ben's statement: A ⊕ B. A=T, B=F, so true.\n- Clara's statement: A ↔ C. A=T, C=T, so true.\n\nSo, all three statements are true. So, all three are truthful. But let's check if that's consistent.\n\nIf all three are truthful:\n\n- Alice's statement: If Ben is truthful, then Clara is lying. But Ben is truthful and Clara is truthful, so Alice's statement is false. Contradiction. So, Combination 3 is invalid.\n\nSo, only Combination 4 is valid.\n\nWait, but earlier, I thought Combination 4 was valid, but let me check again.\n\nCombination 4: A=T, B=F, C=F.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=F, so implication is true.\n- Ben's statement: A ⊕ B. A=T, B=F, so true.\n- Clara's statement: A ↔ C. A=T, C=F, so false.\n\nSo, all three statements are true except Clara's. But since Clara is lying, her statement is false, which is consistent.\n\nWait, but Ben's statement is true, which is consistent because Ben is truthful.\n\nSo, Combination 4 is consistent.\n\nBut earlier, I thought Combination 2 was invalid because Ben's statement would be false, but in Combination 2, Ben is truthful, so his statement must be true. So, Combination 2 is invalid.\n\nSo, only Combination 4 is valid.\n\nWait, but let me check Combination 1 again.\n\nCombination 1: A=T, B=T, C=T.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=T, C=T, so true.\n- Ben's statement: A ⊕ B. A=T, B=T, so false.\n- Clara's statement: A ↔ C. A=T, C=T, so true.\n\nSo, Alice and Clara are truthful, Ben is lying. Let's check consistency.\n\nIf A=T, B=T, C=T.\n\n- Alice's statement: If Ben is truthful, then Clara is lying. But Ben is truthful and Clara is truthful, so Alice's statement is false. Contradiction. So, Combination 1 is invalid.\n\nSo, only Combination 4 is valid.\n\nWait, but earlier, I thought Combination 4 was valid, but let me check if there's another combination.\n\nWait, let me think about Combination 5: A=F, B=T, C=T.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=T, C=T, so true.\n- Ben's statement: A ⊕ B. A=F, B=T, so true.\n- Clara's statement: A ↔ C. A=F, C=T, so false.\n\nSo, all three statements are true except Clara's. But since Clara is lying, her statement is false, which is consistent.\n\nBut wait, in Combination 5, A=F, B=T, C=T.\n\n- Alice's statement: If Ben is truthful, then Clara is lying. Ben is truthful, Clara is truthful. So, Alice's statement is false. Contradiction. So, Combination 5 is invalid.\n\nSimilarly, Combination 6: A=F, B=T, C=F.\n\n- Alice's statement: B → C. B=T, C=F, so false.\n- Ben's statement: A ⊕ B. A