    "chain": "Alright, so I've got this logic puzzle here with four colleagues: Diana, Erik, Fiona, and George. Each of them makes a statement about who will receive a promotion. The catch is that exactly three of these four statements are true, and exactly one is false. I need to figure out who gets the promotion based on their statements. Hmm, okay, let's break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me list out the statements again to have them clear in my mind:\n\n1. Diana says: \"Either Erik or Fiona will get the promotion, but not both.\"\n2. Erik says: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\"\n3. Fiona says: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\"\n4. George says: \"Exactly two people will receive promotions.\"\n\nSo, four statements, three true, one false. I need to figure out who gets the promotion. Let's denote the four people as D, E, F, G for Diana, Erik, Fiona, and George respectively. Each can either get a promotion (T) or not (F). So, we're dealing with four variables: D, E, F, G, each can be T or F.\n\nGiven that exactly three statements are true and one is false, I need to find the combination of T/F assignments that satisfies this condition.\n\nLet me start by analyzing each statement one by one.\n\n**Diana's Statement: \"Either Erik or Fiona will get the promotion, but not both.\"**\n\nThis is an exclusive OR (XOR) statement. So, Diana is saying that exactly one of E or F gets a promotion. So, Diana is claiming that E and F are different. So, Diana is saying E XOR F is true.\n\n**Erik's Statement: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\"**\n\nThis is a conditional statement. In logic terms, this is E → ¬G. Remember, in logic, an implication P → Q is only false when P is true and Q is false. So, if E is true, then G must be false. If E is false, the implication is automatically true regardless of G.\n\n**Fiona's Statement: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\"**\n\nThis is an OR statement with a twist. It's saying (D ∧ F) ∨ (¬D ∧ ¬F). So, either both Diana and Fiona get promotions, or neither of them do. So, Fiona is stating that D and F are both true or both false.\n\n**George's Statement: \"Exactly two people will receive promotions.\"**\n\nThis is a straightforward statement. George is claiming that exactly two of the four will get promotions. So, exactly two Ts and two Fs.\n\nNow, since exactly three statements are true and one is false, I need to figure out which statement is false and see if the others can hold.\n\nLet me consider each possibility of which statement is false and see if the remaining three can be true.\n\n**Case 1: Diana's statement is false.**\n\nIf Diana's statement is false, then it's not the case that either E or F gets a promotion, but not both. So, Diana is saying E XOR F is true, so if Diana is false, E XOR F is false. That means E and F are both true or both false.\n\nSo, in this case, Diana is lying, so her statement is false. Then, the other three statements must be true.\n\nLet's see what that implies.\n\nIf E and F are both true or both false.\n\nLet's consider both possibilities:\n\n**Subcase 1a: E and F are both true.**\n\nSo, E = T, F = T.\n\nNow, let's look at the other statements.\n\n**Erik's Statement: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\"**\n\nSince E is true, according to this statement, G must be false. So, G = F.\n\n**Fiona's Statement: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\"**\n\nSince D is false (because Diana is lying), and F is true, the statement is (F) ∨ (¬D ∧ ¬F). Since F is true, the entire statement is true. So, Fiona is telling the truth.\n\n**George's Statement: \"Exactly two people will receive promotions.\"**\n\nBut in this case, E and F are both true, so that's two promotions. But George is claiming exactly two, so George is telling the truth.\n\nSo, in this subcase, Diana is lying, and the other three statements are true. So, this seems possible.\n\nBut let's check the total number of promotions: E and F are both true, so two promotions. George is saying exactly two, so that's consistent. So, this is a possible scenario.\n\nBut let's check the other possibility.\n\n**Subcase 1b: E and F are both false.**\n\nSo, E = F = F.\n\nNow, let's look at the other statements.\n\n**Erik's Statement: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\"**\n\nSince E is false, the implication is automatically true, regardless of G. So, regardless of G, this statement is true.\n\n**Fiona's Statement: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\"**\n\nSince D is false (Diana is lying), and F is false, the statement is (¬D ∧ ¬F) which is true. So, Fiona is telling the truth.\n\n**George's Statement: \"Exactly two people will receive promotions.\"**\n\nBut in this case, E and F are both false, so only D and G could be true. So, if D and G are both true, that's two promotions. If D is true and G is false, that's one promotion. If D is false and G is true, that's one promotion. If both D and G are false, that's zero promotions.\n\nBut George is claiming exactly two promotions. So, in this case, if D and G are both true, George is correct. But if D is true and G is false, George is wrong. Similarly, if D is false and G is true, George is wrong. If both are false, George is wrong.\n\nBut in this subcase, E and F are both false, so D and G can be either both true, or one true and one false, or both false.\n\nBut George is claiming exactly two promotions. So, if D and G are both true, George is correct. If D is true and G is false, George is wrong. If D is false and G is true, George is wrong. If both are false, George is wrong.\n\nBut in this subcase, since E and F are both false, and we don't know about D and G. So, George's statement could be true or false depending on D and G.\n\nBut in this case, since we have Diana lying, and the other three statements are true, let's see:\n\nIf D is true and G is true, then George is correct. So, that would make three truths and one lie (Diana). So, that's a possible scenario.\n\nAlternatively, if D is true and G is false, then George is wrong, which would make three lies, which is not allowed.\n\nSimilarly, if D is false and G is true, George is wrong, which is also three lies.\n\nIf both D and G are false, George is wrong, which is three lies.\n\nSo, in this subcase, if E and F are both false, then for George's statement to be true, D and G must both be true. So, that would make three truths (E, F, D) and one lie (George). So, that's another possible scenario.\n\nWait, but in this subcase, E and F are both false, so if D and G are both true, that's two promotions (D and G), which is exactly two, so George is correct. So, that's a valid scenario.\n\nSo, in this case, Diana is lying, and the other three statements are true. So, this is another possible scenario.\n\nSo, in Case 1, where Diana's statement is false, we have two possibilities:\n\n- Subcase 1a: E and F are both true, leading to George being correct, so two promotions (E and F), and George is correct.\n\n- Subcase 1b: E and F are both false, leading to D and G both true, so two promotions (D and G), and George is correct.\n\nSo, both subcases are possible. So, Diana's statement being false can lead to two different scenarios.\n\nBut wait, let's check if these scenarios are consistent with the other statements.\n\nIn Subcase 1a: E and F are both true. So, two promotions. Then, George is correct, so exactly two promotions. So, that's consistent.\n\nIn Subcase 1b: E and F are both false. So, two non-promotions. Then, D and G must both be true for George to be correct. So, that's consistent.\n\nSo, both subcases are possible if Diana's statement is false.\n\nBut wait, let's check if these scenarios are possible without contradictions.\n\nIn Subcase 1a: E and F are both true. So, two promotions. Then, George is correct, so exactly two promotions. So, that