      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "Alright, so I've got this logic puzzle here with four colleagues: Diana, Erik, Fiona, and George. Each of them makes a statement about who will receive a promotion. The catch is that exactly three of these four statements are true, and exactly one is false. I need to figure out who gets the promotion based on their statements. Hmm, okay, let's break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me list out the statements again to have them clear in my mind:\n\n1. Diana says: \"Either Erik or Fiona will get the promotion, but not both.\"\n2. Erik says: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\"\n3. Fiona says: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\"\n4. George says: \"Exactly two people will receive promotions.\"\n\nSo, four statements, three true, one false. I need to figure out who gets the promotion."
      },
      {
        "label": "adding-knowledge",
        "text": "Let's denote the four people as D, E, F, G for Diana, Erik, Fiona, and George respectively. Each can either get a promotion (T) or not (F). So, we're dealing with four variables: D, E, F, G, each can be T or F.\n\nGiven that exactly three statements are true and one is false, I need to find the combination of T/F assignments that satisfies this condition."
      },
